  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 231 PM CDT Thu Jul 30 2026\n\n\nTonight...\n\nSteady southerly winds gusting as high as 20MPH early this afternoon \nhave helped advect in a warmer and more humid airmass today (70s to \nlow 80s dewpoints). Despite the excessive cloud coverage for most of \nthe day so far, temperatures still look to remain mostly on track to \nreach the mid 80s to low 90s for much of central Nebraska. \nTemperatures across some north central Kansas locations and far \nsouthwest Nebraska areas may peak as high as the mid to upper 90s \ntoday. The main story, however, will be of the potential for severe \nweather this evening.  \n\nAn upper-level shortwave trough ejecting out from eastern \nColorado/Wyoming this afternoon has lead to some mid-level pressure \nfalls as well as some vorticity and cold air advection across the \nregion. The warming temperatures at the surface paired with cooling \naloft has helped increase instability/buoyancy (up to 2,000-\n3,500J/kg of MUCAPE). A 1004mb surface low deepening across central \nNebraska this evening will help steer frontogenesis across central \nNebraska and central Kansas. A cold front emerging from this process \nwill in turn help develop a cluster of storms. Storms organizing \ninto a line this afternoon, will be expected to drive southeast \nthrough much of the area between mainly 7PM and 1AM tonight. As of \n2PM today, this line of storms looks to be already taking shape in \ncherry county in Nebraska based on the latest radar reflectivity \nreturns and the agitated cumulus observed on satellite.  \n\nA few of these storms will have the potential to become strong to \nsevere with damaging winds being the primary concern. Given the \npresence of modest deep layer shear (25-40MPH) with sufficient \ninstability and moisture, the base ingredients will be met for \nsevere storm characteristics. Though hail up to the size of quarters may \nstill be possible within a few of the stronger/taller storms, the \nhigh freezing levels with more unfavorable mid-level lapse rates (5-\n6C/km), should generally keep the hail threat more limited tonight. \nIn addition, though heavy downpours will be expected within several \nof these storms (1.5-2\" PWATS), given their fairly progressive \nnature, the overall flooding threat should remain limited. The Storm \nPrediction Center now includes the full area in a slight risk (level \n2 of 5) for severe weather.\n\n\nFriday through Wednesday... \n\nFollowing the exit of the storms tonight, the forecast remains dry \nuntil Tuesday night (20-50% chances for central Nebraska). This \ndrier pattern will come as southwest U.S. ridging expands northeast \ninto the Central Plains over the weekend. The surface low pulling \neast and away on Friday will leave behind gusty northerly winds that \nmay peak as high as 30-45MPH during the afternoon. Meanwhile, drier \nand relatively \"cooler\" temperatures with keep highs peaking in the \n80s for both Friday and Saturday. Higher pressure eventually \nsettling back in over the weekend will lighten winds with \ndirections becoming southerly again by Sunday. The start of the new \nweek will bring warming temperatures once more with highs returning \nback to the mid 80s and 90s.",
